ESPN salutes the National Association for Stock Car Auto Racing as we look back on 50 years of

02:29:39.490 --> 02:29:43.980
racing and the fastest growing sport in the nation. NASCAR's 50th anniversary celebration

02:29:43.980 --> 02:29:51.510
is brought to you by Coca-Cola. Always the real thing. Always Coca-Cola. In the 1990s,

02:29:51.510 --> 02:29:55.330
the close racing and fast action at Bristol have drawn hundreds of thousands of fans to

02:29:55.330 --> 02:30:00.000
the mountains of eastern Tennessee. But like NASCAR itself, the Speed Palace had its humble

02:30:00.000 --> 02:30:05.740
beginnings. In 1961, Larry Carrier, Carl Moore, and RG Pope opened the Bristol Motor Speedway on

02:30:05.740 --> 02:30:11.640
the side of an old dairy farm. A capacity crowd of 18,000 saw Fred Lorenzen win the first Bristol

02:30:11.640 --> 02:30:17.060
Pole and Jack Smith win the first race. The track remained relatively unchanged through the late

02:30:17.060 --> 02:30:22.000
70s, but with more TV coverage in the 80s, the sport gained more fans and Bristol needed more

02:30:22.000 --> 02:30:29.260
seats. 26,000 seats in the early 80s doubled to nearly 55,000 by 1990, and by the time Bruton

02:30:29.260 --> 02:30:30.000
Smith bought the track

02:30:30.000 --> 02:30:33.840
Back in 1996, capacity was 71,000.

02:30:33.840 --> 02:30:36.900
Perhaps Bristol more than any other track best exemplifies

02:30:36.900 --> 02:30:39.880
the explosion of NASCAR racing across the U.S.

02:30:39.880 --> 02:30:43.880
Some 135,000 are here now to see NASCAR racing

02:30:43.880 --> 02:30:47.440
in its 50th season.

02:30:47.440 --> 02:30:49.580
And they are loving every minute of it here today.

